{
  "id": "PSI-26",
  "summary_plain": "Solid materials like fabric and plastics were burned inside the Microgravity Science Glovebox on the International Space Station to study how they catch fire and how flames spread without gravity. Researchers observed how air flow speed and nitrogen affect flame growth and extinction. This research helps improve spacecraft materials selection and fire safety designs for future missions.",
  "summary_expert": "The BASS investigation examined burning and extinction characteristics of solid fuels including custom cotton-fiberglass fabric, PMMA spheres, porous wax slabs, and wax candles inside the SPICE flow duct aboard the ISS. Tests evaluated the effects of ambient air flow speeds ranging from 0 to 40 cm/s in concurrent and opposed flow configurations, as well as nitrogen extinguishment. Results demonstrated that concurrent-flow flame spread rate increased linearly with flow speed and that flammability boundaries in microgravity exhibit a U-shaped quenching extinction curve at low flow velocities.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "text": "Materials in microgravity, with adequate ventilation, burn as well if not better than the same material in normal gravity with other conditions being identical.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-26",
        "doi": "10.60555/58h4-dy80",
        "file": "metadata/PSI-26.json#hypothesis",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Concurrent-flow flame spread rate increases linearly with flow speed, and linear curve fits are shown, with spread rate being width-dependent for the two widths studied.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-26",
        "doi": "10.60555/58h4-dy80",
        "file": "documents/PSI-26/PSI-26_Science Documents_FlammabilityAspectsofFabric_USNationalCombustion.2013.docx",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Opposed-flow flame spread rate increases with flow to a maximum when the flow speed is around 10 to 15 cm/s and then decreases at higher speeds.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-26",
        "doi": "10.60555/58h4-dy80",
        "file": "documents/PSI-26/PSI-26_Science Documents_FlammabilityAspectsofFabric_USNationalCombustion.2013.docx",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "For concurrent flow, extinction was observed at a low but finite flow speed of less than 1 cm/s.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-26",
        "doi": "10.60555/58h4-dy80",
        "file": "documents/PSI-26/PSI-26_Science Documents_FlammabilityAspectsofFabric_USNationalCombustion.2013.docx",
        "page": null
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Current findings show that local application of suppressant is not adequate for flame extinguishment, as the local jet entrains air and sustains the flame even when the ambient airflow is turned off.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "REF",
        "doi": "10.60555/58h4-dy80",
        "file": "reference/NASA_Researchers_Guide_ISS_Combustion_Science_2015.pdf",
        "page": 31
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    }
